WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
 
this is not  an everyday arrest on our streets  instead  it's police manhandling and detaining a  father  right beside his dying daughter in  hospital  zenith was born with a rare genetic  illness which the family didn't know  about  until she was three in july last year  the six-year-old's health  rapidly deteriorated she was admitted to  hospital  struggling to breathe and put on life  support  but her parents both doctors say the  staff at the hospital in the north of  england  weren't giving her the correct treatment  they never listened to  to us they would never provide you know  explanations for their actions  because with my medical background  i'm an adult chest consultant and the  disease then  was suffering is is not uncommon  in adult population so i was able to  ask you know leading questions  in august 2019 the medics looking after  critically ill xenib  decided it was time to end her treatment  and she should be allowed to die  her parents insisted this wasn't the  right thing to do  leading to a bitter breakdown in the  relationship with the doctors treating  her  i believe that because our relationship  had deteriorated over the past 18 months  they had finally come to a decision that  this was it and they had made a decision  dr abbasi clung to his daughter's  bedside  refusing to leave denying the doctors  from removing the ventilator  which was keeping xenoblade  unfortunately we need to speak to you  outside  just been told about half an hour ago  that they are going to take the tube out  and our daughter is going to die  it meant police were called and what  unfolded  was far from civil i've got chest pain i  will take it in even  i've got medicine in my pocket i want to  have some  i've got medicine in my pocket if you  sit up reasonably on the chair we'll get  your medication  i can't i'm feeling dizzy dr abbasi  suffered from a heart attack  and was taken to a e i actually thought  that he was gonna die  and at one point i said to the nurses i  said am i gonna be  organizing two funerals they've told us  in a statement they've said we responded  to a call  of a man being violent and abusive  towards staff  and that he had assaulted a consultant  was that right  so if that was right why didn't they  come and arrest me  but then when they asked you to leave  the bed and go outside  why did you not comply to that because i  knew that  if i left the bed space i would not be  allowed to come back  we're human beings we're parents we're  told our daughter's going to die  and we're shouted at we were treated we  were treated like scum  the force also said while we recognize  this was a very distressing time  for him and his family our duty was to  ensure  the safety of all those present we have  reviewed the body-worn footage  from the incident which sets out a very  different picture to the limited version  of events  which have been presented to us the nhs  trust  which treated zineb offered their  heartfelt sympathies  and condolences to the family and they  told us their first priority  was to always act in the best interest  of their patients  in a statement they said on very rare  occasions  when there is a risk to the safety of  our staff or obstruction  and interference with the delivery of  care and treatment  it is necessary for us to seek help from  the police  following the incident the nhs trust  applied to the high court  for permission to take zenib off the  ventilator but in mid-september  just three days before the hearing was  due to start she died  i want the world to see so that it is  not allowed to happen anymore  the family are now taking legal action  against the police and the hospital  they're being supported by christian  concern a religious organization  but whoever is right or wrong in this  bitter feud  the footage of police dragging  distraught parents away from their dying  child  is an image many will struggle to forget  insaam rasheed sky news

